WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:23.670
CHIPOTLE’S SHADY HISTORY:
Since 2008, Chipotle has had 10 scandals regarding

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:28.450
food poisoning which has affected thousands
of people spreading diseases like Hepatitis

00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:33.120
A, Norovirus, E. Coli, Salmonella, and Clostridium
Perfringens.

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:37.590
And those are just the outbreaks that made
it to the news.

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:41.780
Many of you probably know people who were
food poisonined after eating at Chipotle,

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:44.190
or maybe it’s even happened to you.

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:48.042
Yet, despite all these red flags, Chipotle
is doing better than ever.

00:00:48.042 --> 00:00:55.100
In the last 12 month period, Chipotle brought
in $8.4 billion in revenue and ended up profiting

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:56.670
$800 million.

00:00:56.670 --> 00:01:01.510
Similarly, they’ve expanded to over 3000
locations across the US.

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:05.800
It’s not just their fundamentals that have
been doing well either.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:10.700
Chipotle stock has also held up extraordinarily
well throughout the recession being down only

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:12.610
a mere 20%.

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:18.479
This pegs Chipotles valuation at $41 billion
which nearly makes them a top 10 food brand

00:01:18.479 --> 00:01:20.110
in terms of market cap.

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:24.700
For perspective, that’s over 3 times larger
than Domino’s Pizza.

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:28.330
Given their popularity, you would think that
they addressing their food poisoning concerns

00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:33.119
would be their top priority, and according
to their PR, it is.

00:01:33.119 --> 00:01:37.580
But, when we look at the facts, they don’t
look nearly as favorable for Chipotle.

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:42.160
In fact, It actually looks like they’re
regressing given that their outbreaks tend

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:44.570
to affect more people today.

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:49.369
For example, their first outbreak in 2008
affected 22 customers.

00:01:49.369 --> 00:01:54.690
Conversely, an outbreak in 2018 ended up affecting
700 people.

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:59.320
At first glance, you might think that this
is somewhat expected for a chain as large

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:00.320
as Chipotle.

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:05.590
When you’re serving that much food, you’re
bound to serve some that gets people sick.

00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:08.350
But, this is not actually true.

00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:13.790
McDonald’s, for example, has over 10 times
the number of locations all over the world.

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:18.860
Yet, their Wikipedia page only mentions the
word “outbreak” once and even that was

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:22.100
in the context of describing the Coronavirus
outbreak.

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:28.160
Conversely, Chipotle’s wikipedia page mentions
the work outbreak a whopping 91 times.

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:32.680
What makes this even more confusing is that
Chipotle uses much of the same food supply

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:34.680
chain as McDonalds.

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:40.720
So, what in the world is going on at Chipotle
and why do so many people choose to eat there

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:42.140
anyway.

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:48.860
BEFORE CHIPOTLE’S ISSUES:
To understand what’s going wrong at Chipotle,

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:53.940
we first have to take a look at their origin
because there was a point in time when Chipotle

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:58.890
wasn’t synonymous with food poisoning, granted
it’s been quite some time.

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:04.370
The story of Chipotle dates back to 1993 to
a man named Steve Ells.

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:08.819
Steve was a classically trained chef who had
a few years of experience working in the traditional

00:03:08.819 --> 00:03:10.690
restaurant industry as a sous chef.

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:15.680
While this gig gave Steve some experience,
he knew from day one that he wanted to open

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:17.580
a fine dining restaurant.

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:21.159
Jumping to that from just being a sous chef
was quite a leap though.

00:03:21.159 --> 00:03:26.569
So, at age 28, Steve borrowed $85,000 from
his friends and family and opened a small

00:03:26.569 --> 00:03:29.220
burrito shop instead in Denver Colorado.

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:34.620
Initially, he estimated that he needed to
sell atleast 107 burritos per day to be profitable,

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:36.650
but ended up crushing this number.

00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:41.830
By the end of the first month itself, Steve
was selling over a 1000 burritos per day.

00:03:41.830 --> 00:03:46.720
This strong reception gave Steve the confidence
to take out an SBA loan and open additional

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:48.370
restaurants in Colorado.

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:53.010
We can’t give Steve all the credit though
as he had substantial help from his father.

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:56.720
You see, Steve’s father was a wealthy pharmaceutical
executive.

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:01.319
And while he was hesitant to invest at ground
zero, once he saw these results, he became

00:04:01.319 --> 00:04:05.090
a massive investor putting in $1.5 million.

00:04:05.090 --> 00:04:09.349
Despite having access to all this capital,
Steve was in no rush to open up a bunch of

00:04:09.349 --> 00:04:12.959
restaurants or expand outside of the state
for two reasons.

00:04:12.959 --> 00:04:17.959
For one, Steve strongly believed in keeping
the business simple and straightforward.

00:04:17.959 --> 00:04:22.030
And this is very much a core principle of
Chipotle to this day.

00:04:22.030 --> 00:04:24.889
I mean, this is basically their entire menu.

00:04:24.889 --> 00:04:30.259
And two, turning Chipotle into a nationwide
giant was never Steve’s vision.

00:04:30.259 --> 00:04:34.139
His goal was to simply get some experience
running Chipotle before switching over to

00:04:34.139 --> 00:04:35.139
fine dining.

00:04:35.139 --> 00:04:40.680
But, by 1998, it was becoming extremely difficult
to stick to this original vision simply due

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:42.919
to how well Chipotle was doing.

00:04:42.919 --> 00:04:48.259
Instead of resisting, Steve would simply embrace
the change and go all in on Chipotle, opening

00:04:48.259 --> 00:04:51.270
their first restaurant outside of Colorado
in 1998.

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:57.050
Now that Chipotle was no longer just a local
sensation, bigger investors would become interested,

00:04:57.050 --> 00:04:58.740
particularly McDonalds.

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:03.290
McDonalds started off with a small stake,
but they would end up making Chipotle into

00:05:03.290 --> 00:05:04.960
who they are today.

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:11.270
Over the next 7 years, McDonalds helped Chipotle
grow from 16 to locations to over 500 locations

00:05:11.270 --> 00:05:18.190
by investing a whopping $360 million which
gave them a 90% stake in the company.

00:05:18.190 --> 00:05:22.580
But, money wasn’t even the biggest asset
that McDonalds brought to the table.

00:05:22.580 --> 00:05:26.490
Their primary contribution was of course their
experience.

00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:30.550
While McDonalds gets a bad rep for selling
highly processed junk food, one thing you

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:34.639
can’t argue with is that they're exceptional
at selling said food.

00:05:34.639 --> 00:05:39.699
They’ve honed in everything from marketing
and franchising to the supply chain and food

00:05:39.699 --> 00:05:40.740
safety.

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:45.370
And this expertise was very much evident in
Chiptole’s flawless growth from 16 stores

00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:47.169
to 500 stores.

00:05:47.169 --> 00:05:52.310
But, unfortunately for Chipotle, McDonald’s
wouldn’t stick around forever.

00:05:52.310 --> 00:05:58.949
AWAY WITH MCDONALDS:
While McDonald’s was crushing it with Chipotle,

00:05:58.949 --> 00:06:02.569
the same could not be said about their own
business.

00:06:02.569 --> 00:06:08.080
During the dotcom crash, McDonald’s stock
would sell of 75% and it would take them 8

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:10.509
years to return to their former highs.

00:06:10.509 --> 00:06:15.259
While much of this was simply due to the state
of the economy, McDonald’s executives decided

00:06:15.259 --> 00:06:19.900
that they had to stop spending so much effort
on relatively small investments like Chipotle

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:22.919
and instead focus on their own growth.

00:06:22.919 --> 00:06:29.169
So, in 2006, they decided to take Chipotle
public and sell off their entire stake.

00:06:29.169 --> 00:06:33.289
Despite all this selling pressure, Chipotle
went on to have an extremely successfully

00:06:33.289 --> 00:06:34.699
IPO.

00:06:34.699 --> 00:06:40.690
Before going public, Chipotle actually raised
their IPO price twice, but even with that,

00:06:40.690 --> 00:06:45.020
Chipotle ended up rocketing 100% during their
first day as a public company.

00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:50.349
This made Chipotle the second best restaurant
IPO since the dotcom crash.

00:06:50.349 --> 00:06:54.069
This strong support from investors made it
seemed like Chipotle would have a smooth time

00:06:54.069 --> 00:06:55.800
without McDonald’s.

00:06:55.800 --> 00:07:00.479
But, almost immediately, you could see the
result.

00:07:00.479 --> 00:07:05.569
Just two years later, Chipotle would have
their first notable health crisis in March

00:07:05.569 --> 00:07:06.569
of 2008.

00:07:06.569 --> 00:07:12.620
A Chipotle location in La Mesa, California
would be linked to 22 cases of Hepatitis A.

00:07:12.620 --> 00:07:16.770
Just one month later, Chipotle would have
yet another outbreak.

00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:22.440
This time the disease was Norovirus and it
ended up infecting 440 people.

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:26.060
So, what in the world happened within these
24 months?

00:07:26.060 --> 00:07:30.849
Well, one of the most notable points was Chipotle’s
trend towards decentralization.

00:07:30.849 --> 00:07:35.680
I don’t think you’d be surprised to hear
that McDonald’s was a strong proponent of

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:37.680
centralized operations.

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:41.710
Virtually all of their ingredients come from
mass distributors like Keystone Foods.

00:07:41.710 --> 00:07:46.530
Every year, Keystone Foods alone delivers
150 million pounds of beef, 300 million pounds

00:07:46.530 --> 00:07:49.550
of chicken, and 15 million pounds of fish
to McDonalds.

00:07:49.550 --> 00:07:53.729
While this style of supply chain raises a
lot of questions regarding animal treatment,

00:07:53.729 --> 00:07:58.270
perservatives, and GMOs, one thing that’s
not in question is consistency.

00:07:58.270 --> 00:08:03.509
When all of your food comes from one location,
it’s all gonna be the same.

00:08:03.509 --> 00:08:07.830
More importantly though, a simplified supply
chain makes it easy to maintain quality and

00:08:07.830 --> 00:08:09.790
identify issues quickly.

00:08:09.790 --> 00:08:13.610
Given that much of Chipotle’s entire brand
is built around simplicity, you would think

00:08:13.610 --> 00:08:18.120
that Steve would stick to a centralized supply
chain, but he actually had other plans.

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:23.539
You see, Steve had caught sense of a new food
trend which pushed people to ditch junk food

00:08:23.539 --> 00:08:24.889
and eat healthier.

00:08:24.889 --> 00:08:29.110
Being an opportunistic business man, Steve
decided to jump onto this trend of producing

00:08:29.110 --> 00:08:31.860
quote on quote organic locally sourced food.

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:36.880
Looking back, this was a perfect choice in
terms of their marketing and branding, and

00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:38.860
it’s also the ethical choice.

00:08:38.860 --> 00:08:43.180
But, this turned out to be a nightmare in
terms of quality control.

00:08:43.180 --> 00:08:49.670
SUPPLY CHAIN HELL:
Today, Chipotle tries to source as many ingredients

00:08:49.670 --> 00:08:52.720
as possible from within a 350 mile radius.

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:57.700
While this locally grown produce sounds great
on paper, there’s a reason that mega food

00:08:57.700 --> 00:09:00.180
corporations shy away from this idea.

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:04.770
It’s because it’s virtually impossible
to maintain standards across thousands of

00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:08.920
suppliers, and Chipotle would figure this
out the hard way.

00:09:08.920 --> 00:09:13.680
This decentralized supply chain also put a
lot of strain on Chipotle’s stores because

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:18.990
their stores were built to operate in the
exact opposite manner: with simplicity.

00:09:18.990 --> 00:09:23.940
Chipotle locations don’t have any freezers,
microwaves, or even can openers.

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:27.170
This means that the staff have to take on
a lot of the responsibility when it comes

00:09:27.170 --> 00:09:28.930
to preparing the food.

00:09:28.930 --> 00:09:34.089
Given that it’s a restaurant, you would
think that this would be a given, but it’s

00:09:34.089 --> 00:09:35.089
not.

00:09:35.089 --> 00:09:38.790
Most fast food places structure their business
such that fast food workers don’t have to

00:09:38.790 --> 00:09:40.620
do much preparation.

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:44.180
Basically everything comes premade meaning
that the workers simply have to heat up the

00:09:44.180 --> 00:09:45.860
food or assemble it.

00:09:45.860 --> 00:09:49.260
Chipotle workers on the other hand are literally
given raw ingredients.

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:52.660
I mean, they literally make the food in front
of you.

00:09:52.660 --> 00:09:57.100
This would be ok if we were talking about
an actual sit down restaurant filled with

00:09:57.100 --> 00:09:58.440
experienced staff.

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:03.770
But, most Chipotle locations are run by young
adults and high school students, so there’s

00:10:03.770 --> 00:10:05.370
a massive learning curve.

00:10:05.370 --> 00:10:11.290
In 2015 and 2016, during the worst of Chipotle’s
outbreaks, Chipotle actually spent a lot of

00:10:11.290 --> 00:10:13.860
effort on strengthening their training programs.

00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:19.129
But, this quickly led them to yet another
issue: employee turnover.

00:10:19.129 --> 00:10:22.480
Just as employees start to get the hang of
things, they leave.

00:10:22.480 --> 00:10:27.480
In 2021, Chipotle’s turnover rate was 194%.

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:32.740
This means that the average employee barely
sticks around for 4 months which is not surprising

00:10:32.740 --> 00:10:35.750
given that it’s a near minimum wage job.

00:10:35.750 --> 00:10:40.910
Chipotle has tried to combat this by handing
out cash and stock bonuses, offering maternity/paternity

00:10:40.910 --> 00:10:44.760
coverage, and even offering life insurance
and short term disability.

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:50.579
But, none of this can change the fundamental
fact that in the end, it’s still a fast

00:10:50.579 --> 00:10:51.579
food job.

00:10:51.579 --> 00:10:55.070
And this is really the story of Chipotle as
a whole.

00:10:55.070 --> 00:10:59.160
They’re trying to do all these great things
like sourcing local food and promoting healthier

00:10:59.160 --> 00:11:04.139
options, but they’re still a fast food place
and they have to deal with all of the limitations

00:11:04.139 --> 00:11:06.020
that come along with that.

00:11:06.020 --> 00:11:09.690
And these issues are only made worse by seemingly
negligent leadership.

00:11:09.690 --> 00:11:14.839
While Steve started Chipotle with a great
passion for food, it seems that over the years,

00:11:14.839 --> 00:11:17.350
he has become much more interested in the
money.

00:11:17.350 --> 00:11:22.270
In fact, Chipotle is infamous for paying their
executives ridiculous sums of money.

00:11:22.270 --> 00:11:28.300
Back in the early 2010s, Chipotle paid out
$300 million to their co CEOs.

00:11:28.300 --> 00:11:33.410
These guys were earning FAANG CEO type salaries
even though Chipotle was pulling in less than

00:11:33.410 --> 00:11:37.529
1% of the revenue of companies like Apple
or Google.

00:11:37.529 --> 00:11:41.530
Not to mention, their profit margins were
way lower as well.

00:11:41.530 --> 00:11:45.600
Shareholders did eventually put their foot
down and refused to approve such compensation

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:51.650
packages, but this just goes to show where
their executives priorities really were.

00:11:51.650 --> 00:11:57.380
THE STATE OF CHIPOTLE:
Considering all this, why in the world do

00:11:57.380 --> 00:12:00.050
people continue to eat at Chipotle?

00:12:00.050 --> 00:12:02.100
Well, the answer is simple.

00:12:02.100 --> 00:12:08.690
As this quora user puts it Chipotle actually
serves real food or atleast realer food than

00:12:08.690 --> 00:12:10.360
other fast food joints.

00:12:10.360 --> 00:12:16.830
Real food comes with real flavors that create
a unique appeal, but they also come with real

00:12:16.830 --> 00:12:17.830
risk.

00:12:17.830 --> 00:12:22.070
The only way to avoid this risk is to cook
yourself, spend a fortune on eating out, or

00:12:22.070 --> 00:12:23.829
eat highly processed foods.

00:12:23.829 --> 00:12:29.670
And given that most people are one, too lazy
to cook themselves, two, not rich enough to

00:12:29.670 --> 00:12:34.420
eat out at 5 star restaurants regularly, and
three want something more than McDonalds or

00:12:34.420 --> 00:12:39.649
Burger King junk, they flock to something
in the middle which just so happens to be

00:12:39.649 --> 00:12:40.649
Chipotle.

00:12:40.649 --> 00:12:44.860
So, Chipotle’s greatest strength is also
their biggest weakness.

00:12:44.860 --> 00:12:47.480
And they only have two options moving forward.

00:12:47.480 --> 00:12:53.079
They can recentralize like the legacy competition,
or they can try to fix what they already have.

00:12:53.079 --> 00:12:57.930
It’s clear which side Chipotle has chosen,
and looking at their fundamentals, it’s

00:12:57.930 --> 00:12:59.970
working out just fine.

